<!--
  HEALTH - the phone graph view.

  Min/max/average for each of the five measured types (heart rate, blood
  oxygen, HRV, steps, sleep), by hour within a day and by day over a week, a
  month or three months, from whatever history the local store has accumulated.

  Reached from Settings (settings-hub-page.xml), the same route as Active apps
  and every other round-5 setting. Since audit F1 the same graphs are also
  Health's phone view on main-page, so the body lives in health-body.xml and
  this page is only its ActionBar host; the Settings route goes with the
  Settings rework.

  TWO LAYOUTS, ONE PAGE. Both stack the chart over the readout; what differs
  between them is now only how much height the chart gets (see chartHeight in
  health-view-model.ts). Expanded used to put the readout in a fixed 300dp side
  column - that column was removed on Chris's 2026-09-10 review so the chart can
  use the full window width.

  They remain two bodies rather than one because the compact/expanded split
  still decides the chart height, and because they share one grid cell rather
  than being separate pages: unfolding the phone is not a navigation the user
  performed, and a page swap would leave a back-stack entry behind a screen they
  never chose to leave. That is the same reasoning main-page.xml already uses
  for its three bodies, and the same fold signal decides all of them.

  THE LAYOUT LINE AT THE BOTTOM IS NOT DEBUG LEFTOVER. It prints the width, the
  posture and the resulting display class, so a screenshot of this page is
  evidence about the fold question rather than an assertion about it. The Fold-7
  responsive behaviour has been an open item since the emulator dispatch and
  this is the first surface actually exercising it.
-->
<Page xmlns="http://schemas.nativescript.org/tns.xsd" xmlns:ui="phone-ui"
    navigatingTo="navigatingTo" loaded="loaded" unloaded="unloaded">

    <ActionBar title="Health" />

    <ui:health-body />

</Page>
